13:58:59 run E38 20260919-135859: E37 could not resolve the tool's FPS cost because the noise lives BETWEEN rig loads: two off_baseline replicates at effectively identical load (53.5 vs 53.9 units) differed by 16.5%, which swamps any plausible cost of a few Lua jobs. More replicates would not fix that; a different design does. E38 toggles the tool's JOBS on and off in alternating 8,000-tick blocks INSIDE a single replicate, so on-samples and off-samples share one rig load, one save, one thermal state and one population trajectory, and the between-load variance differences away. The scheduler is toggled directly through repeat-util rather than through disableSched(), because disableSched() also runs CAVERN.restoreAll() and writes config to disk - a per-toggle cost that would be charged to whichever phase followed it. The measured frame rate belongs to the interval just ended, so each receipt records the state in force DURING that interval, not the state it is about to switch to. A sham arm runs the identical block bookkeeping while never touching the scheduler: it measures what the method itself appears to find when there is nothing to find.
